WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.400
-Welcome back to the program.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.801
-Those are giant numbers --
10 million albums.

00:00:06.801 --> 00:00:08.100
-I actually didn't know that.
-When you were growing up,

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.667
did you go, "Yeah,
this is what I want to be.

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:10.934
I want to be a giant pop star"?

00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:12.667
-Literally, it's like
the only thing

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:15.067
I've ever wanted
for my entire life.

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:16.667
I remember, like,
some of my earliest memories

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:18.434
is, I would make my parents --

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:19.901
We had, like, curtains
like this in their bedroom,

00:00:19.901 --> 00:00:22.701
and I would close the curtains,
hide behind the curtains,

00:00:22.701 --> 00:00:24.534
and I would make them
introduce me.

00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:28.033
I had a stage name and --
-Who were you?

00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:30.501
-No, I was Troye Sivan,
because that's not my real name.

00:00:30.501 --> 00:00:33.100
I knew from like --
from the age of like 4,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.534
I was like, "I'm Troye Sivan,
and I'm not -- I'm not..."

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:37.567
-No way.
-Yeah. And then I would --

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:38.901
I would come out.
They would introduce me,

00:00:38.901 --> 00:00:41.167
and I'd come out,
and I'd sing for my parents.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.667
-And what would you sing?

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.467
-Like, "Backstreet's Back."

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:46.000
-Yeah.
-You'd do "Backstreet --"

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.701
Yeah.
That's good.

00:00:47.701 --> 00:00:49.434
-I would do "Barbie Girl,"
"Aqua" --

00:00:49.434 --> 00:00:51.334
Like, gay -- gay stuff.

00:00:51.334 --> 00:00:52.734
[ Laughter ]

00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:56.133
Yeah.
[ Laughter continues ]

00:00:56.133 --> 00:00:57.801
-Yeah, okay.
-"Like a Prayer," by Madonna.

00:00:57.801 --> 00:00:58.868
You know what I mean?
Like --

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:00.467
-You would do that --
-My parents knew.

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:01.968
They knew.
-Yeah, they knew, yeah.

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:05.367
-They really knew, yeah.
-The first time you sang

00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:07.167
on Australian TV, you were --
you were 11 years old.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.200
-Yeah.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.534
-What do you remember
about that?

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:10.934
-Well, I looked about 6.

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:14.033
And I remember, at the time,
my barometer

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:18.200
for a successful performance
was making mums cry.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.100
Like, that was always
my biggest goal.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.400
And if I did that,

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.501
then I thought it was
a successful performance.

00:01:23.501 --> 00:01:25.000
And so I would like --

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.501
-You would tug at
the heartstrings?

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:28.133
-Try to, yeah.
I would lay it on so thick.

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:29.801
I was, like,
you know, smiling at the camera,

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:33.934
like, doing whatever I had to do
to win these mums over.

00:01:33.934 --> 00:01:36.100
And it worked. It worked.
-You would make them cry.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.467
We actually found a clip of you.
-You did?

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:41.534
-Yes. This is 11-year-old
Troye Sivan's

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.934
Australian television debut.
Take a look at this.

00:01:43.934 --> 00:01:46.367
-♪ Wake up with the clouds ♪

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:53.200
♪ All far behind me ♪

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.434
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:58.834
♪ Somewhere ♪

00:01:58.834 --> 00:02:02.767
♪ Over ♪

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:08.467
♪ The rainbow ♪

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:10.067
-Oh, come on.

00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:11.767
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:13.334
-[ Mock sniffles ]

00:02:13.334 --> 00:02:15.667
You made me cry a little bit.
You're making me cry.

00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:18.033
-Nice, nice.
-Your new album

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:19.601
comes out on Friday.

00:02:19.601 --> 00:02:20.968
-On Friday.
I can't believe it.

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:22.734
-Big deal. Called
"Something to Give Each Other."

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:25.367
Here's the cover right here.
-Can you show that on TV?

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:26.834
-Yeah, I don't know.

00:02:26.834 --> 00:02:29.133
I didn't even know that I was
posing for the album cover

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:30.801
when I was doing this.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:32.501
Thank you for --
You didn't even ask.

00:02:32.501 --> 00:02:35.501
-At this desk.
-But there you go. Yeah.

00:02:35.501 --> 00:02:37.300
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.200
I look good.
I look good.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.534
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.534 --> 00:02:43.901
It's the first album
in five years?

00:02:43.901 --> 00:02:45.534
-Five years.
Which is insane.

00:02:45.534 --> 00:02:47.567
I think that makes it sound
like I think I'm like Adele

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.267
or something like that
where I can just, like,

00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:51.801
take five years between albums,
but it wasn't intentional.

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:54.734
It was just, like, I started
working on it, and then COVID,

00:02:54.734 --> 00:02:56.701
and then I was, like, filming
a TV show and whatever,

00:02:56.701 --> 00:03:00.234
and it just took a long time.
But I'm genuinely --

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:02.400
Like, this is the most proud
I've ever been of anything.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.334
-Yeah, and you actually
brought us a very cool thing.

00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:08.367
You brought us an exclusive clip
of a song never played anywhere.

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:10.234
We're going to play it
for you tonight.

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:12.200
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.133
-Can I say the title?
-You can say the title.

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:17.667
-The title is "What's the Time
Where You Are?"

00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:20.100
And this is Troye Sivan.
Here we go.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.300
-♪ And are you sure? ♪

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.234
♪ That's just the way
love goes ♪

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:27.434
♪ Now, the only thing
I want to know ♪

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:30.634
♪ Is what's the time
where you are ♪

00:03:30.634 --> 00:03:31.968
♪ You are ♪

00:03:31.968 --> 00:03:34.834
♪ What's the night like
where you are ♪

00:03:34.834 --> 00:03:36.267
♪ You are ♪

00:03:36.267 --> 00:03:39.200
♪ International stranger ♪

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.667
-This is what --
This is --

00:03:41.667 --> 00:03:43.400
[ Music stops ]
-This is like --

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.267
It's a good, fun dancing song.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:46.267
-Thank you.

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:48.400
♪ What's the time
where you are? ♪

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.400
I love it.
-Thank you.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.033
-It's catchy.
-Thank you.

00:03:51.033 --> 00:03:52.334
Is it a very --
Is it a positive --

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:54.334
Is it a pop album?
What kind of --

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:56.033
-Yeah.
So, I didn't know --

00:03:56.033 --> 00:04:00.100
I was like -- I went through
a big breakup in 2019,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.868
and then I was, like,
just really sad for a long time,

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:05.968
and I didn't really feel
much of anything at all.

00:04:05.968 --> 00:04:10.334
And then, I started
to write this album,

00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:11.901
and I was, like,
totally surprised.

00:04:11.901 --> 00:04:14.901
I don't know who's been through
a breakup here, but you know

00:04:14.901 --> 00:04:17.367
that weird moment where,
all of a sudden, you're like,

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:20.100
"Wait. I'm single.
And..."

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.267
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:23.501
And it's like, "Oh. Okay."

00:04:23.501 --> 00:04:26.033
And so I started to --
I felt like I was kind of

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:27.167
like re-meeting myself.

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:28.634
I started to, like,
do weird things,

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:31.067
like, show up to a house party
by myself where I didn't know

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:34.067
anyone, just, like, excited
at the possibilities of life.

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:39.267
And I started to have these,
like, encounters with people.

00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:40.834
And, um...
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.834 --> 00:04:42.367
And I was just really,
really enjoying myself.

00:04:42.367 --> 00:04:44.167
And I think I started
to feel this,

00:04:44.167 --> 00:04:46.100
like, connection
to not just myself,

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.400
but to everyone and to realize,
like, how beautiful,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.501
even a relationship
that lasts a few hours can be.

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.701
[ Laughter ]
And so, um...

00:04:55.701 --> 00:04:57.801
So it was --
-A few minutes sometimes.

00:04:57.801 --> 00:04:58.934
A few --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.934 --> 00:05:01.200
-A few -- few seconds.
Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.033
-Right.
-Yeah. It's pretty, uh --

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.467
It doesn't matter, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:07.133
It doesn't matter at all.
-No, it's not --

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:08.334
It's this
the motion of the ocean.

00:05:08.334 --> 00:05:09.334
You know what I'm saying?
That's the --

00:05:09.334 --> 00:05:10.667
[ Laughter ]

00:05:10.667 --> 00:05:12.267
But you were saying,
"Something to Give Each Other,"

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:13.534
we were talking about it,
you say, like,

00:05:13.534 --> 00:05:16.200
the title of the album
is all these experiences,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.667
no matter what it is --

00:05:17.667 --> 00:05:20.133
I mean, it doesn't have to be
a romantic or a sexual thing,

00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:23.567
but when -- just meeting
new people...

00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:24.701
-Completely.
-...everyone has something

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:26.501
to give each other.
-Exactly right.

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:27.934
And I wrote --
Some of the album

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:30.667
I wrote during COVID when it
was, like, more of an ambition

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:32.300
than it was like --
It was like a fantasy, you know?

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.667
I just sort of --
I was craving connection,

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:37.367
and I was like,
"Wow, everyone has something

00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:39.434
to give each other, whether it
is a couple of seconds,

00:05:39.434 --> 00:05:41.734
in your case or, um...

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:43.534
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:43.534 --> 00:05:45.634
...or a couple of hours in mine.
Like...

00:05:45.634 --> 00:05:48.167
[ Laughter ]

00:05:48.167 --> 00:05:50.234
Yeah, I just thought that was
a really beautiful sentiment,

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:51.801
and I kind of based
the album off of that.

00:05:51.801 --> 00:05:55.634
-Yeah. And maybe, can
we say, possible tour?

00:05:55.634 --> 00:05:57.634
-I think so, yeah.
I just --

00:05:57.634 --> 00:05:59.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:59.534 --> 00:06:02.200
No, like, I'm not --
I'm not announcing it now.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.200
But like, in the future,
there will be a tour.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.133
I think the only reason
I haven't started, like,

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:08.300
actually planning it yet
is, I've been doing,

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.367
like, choreography in
the music videos and stuff...

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:12.067
-Yeah.
-...for the first time.

00:06:12.067 --> 00:06:14.767
And now I feel this pressure

00:06:14.767 --> 00:06:17.234
to be, like,
dancing and singing,

00:06:17.234 --> 00:06:20.367
and I want it to be this, like,
mega, mega, mega pop show.

00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:22.501
When it happens --
You'll do it --

00:06:22.501 --> 00:06:23.667
You'll do it right.
-I want to do it right, yeah.

00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:25.567
-Congratulations.
The album comes out this Friday.

00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:28.267
And possible tour, and come back
when that happens.

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:29.567
We love you, buddy.
You're welcome.

00:06:29.567 --> 00:06:31.234
Always, anytime you want
to come on.

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:32.801
Troye Sivan, everybody.

00:06:32.801 --> 00:06:35.400
"Something to Give Each Other"
comes out on Friday.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.000
We'll be right back with more
"Tonight Show."

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.801
Stick around, everybody!

